WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.139 --> 00:00:08.320
Now the first mesh optimization I'd like to talk about has to deal with system units. Now Unreal Engine 5 uses

2
00:00:08.539 --> 00:00:13.640
centimeters for its unit of measurement, so one Unreal unit equals one centimeter.

3
00:00:14.060 --> 00:00:16.859
The reason that it's so important to make sure that your

4
00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:20.920
digital content creation tool, or your DCC, is set to

5
00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:28.800
centimeters is so that we can avoid any scaling issues between your DCC and your Unreal Engine 5

6
00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:35.520
project. Now here we have the system units of 3D Studio Max showing that we have this set to centimeters, and

7
00:00:36.170 --> 00:00:37.729
while you can

8
00:00:37.729 --> 00:00:39.649
scale your

9
00:00:39.649 --> 00:00:44.570
assets on export, it's better to make sure that you're actually creating them

10
00:00:45.170 --> 00:00:52.649
using centimeters, as this unit of measurement is going to have a lot of effects on how things work in the engine. For example,

11
00:00:53.130 --> 00:00:57.289
importing a really large mesh and then scaling it down could have all types of

12
00:00:57.530 --> 00:01:02.009
repercussion for other systems such as collision, physics, and even nanite.

13
00:01:02.450 --> 00:01:09.760
This is why it's best practice to make sure that everything uses centimeters as your default unit of measurement,

14
00:01:10.079 --> 00:01:13.120
unless your pipeline requires some type of

15
00:01:13.799 --> 00:01:16.920
specific reason for using another unit of measurement.
